@using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection
@using Microsoft.JSInterop
@using global::Backlog.UI.Components.Metrics
@inject IAgentSessionSource Sessions
@inject IDeliveryRunSource Runs
@inject IServiceProvider Services
@inject IJSRuntime JS

@*
    The sessions pane: the Claude and Copilot sessions this environment has a record
    of. The Sessions context's only surface (see .devbook/domain/sessions/).

    It was first built as a second pane on Dev PC Management, which read plausibly
    and was wrong about the boundary. Both are about the machine, but they answer
    different questions in different languages: one is how a PC is configured and
    what an operator can change about it, the other is what has been running on it,
    for how long, and in which repository. This is a list read down and grouped, not
    a set of things to act on, and its vocabulary — session, agent, environment,
    stalled — is its own.

    Like the tools pane, it owns its own loading: the list is the pane's subject, and
    a shell holding it would be holding state about a context it only makes room for.
    The shell decides *whether* there is a sessions pane — the feature flag and the
    tab it opens on, beside the dashboard's overview, stay there — and this decides
    what is in one.

    Two things this pane deliberately is not:

    It is not a monitor. It reads the folders once when it opens and again when
    somebody asks, and there is no timer. A surface that re-read itself every few
    seconds would be a claim to be live that the underlying files do not support: a
    Copilot session leaves no liveness marker at all, so a moving list would move
    without meaning.

    It is not exhaustive, and it says so out loud. Each reader stops at the most
    recent hundred sessions, because a developer's profile holds hundreds of records —
    the machine this was validated on held 842, and 705 of them were Copilot's, which
    as one list was 40,000 pixels of table with the four running sessions somewhere in
    it. When the cap takes anything, the line under the title names both numbers: a
    capped list presented as the whole history is the one thing a cap must not do.

    And one thing it is. It opens on the live sessions — the ones the reading called
    Running or Stalled — because "what is going on right now" is the question somebody
    opens this for, and eight hundred finished records are the answer to a different
    one. What that reading rests on is not the same for both agents, and the reader of
    this pane pays for the difference: Claude writes a file per running session, so its
    live rows are evidence, while Copilot's state is a timeout on top of a folder that
    leaves no liveness marker at all. A Copilot session that is genuinely running but
    has been quiet for over half an hour reads as Finished, and this view does not
    hold it. The view is a filter, and so is Machine beside it — the two controls
    here that remove a row: grouping still moves rows and removes none, which is why
    the badge does not move when the grouping does.

    Machine narrows to one environment, and it is a select where the two strips are
    one-of-many buttons, for the reason the dashboard's machine filter is one: the
    options are however many machines have reported, which is not this pane's
    decision and not a list it can promise stays short. Its options are read off the
    catalog itself, so an option is offered exactly when there are rows behind it,
    and it is named and ordered the way Group by environment names and orders its
    sections — the same derivation, so the two cannot disagree. Like Show, it comes
    back to "All machines" on every open.

    The shell's repository scope reaches this list too — the same chips that narrow
    the task list and the dashboard's overview — and it is the one filter this pane
    does not own a control for, because the control is in the header. A session is
    in scope by either of two repositories: the one its agent recorded, or the one
    this product resolved from its working folder lying inside a registered clone
    (see .devbook/domain/sessions — the two are kept apart because a resolved repository is
    only as good as the clone somebody registered, and the log never writes into a
    gap the agent left). Claude records none, so its sessions are in scope only by
    the second, and a Claude session outside every registered clone — or one that
    arrived from a machine that had registered none — is still left out. The
    subtitle says exactly that rather than letting a reader conclude Claude ran
    nothing. What either repository is called on the wire — an owner/name, or an
    alias — is the shell's to resolve, which is why the mapping is handed in beside
    the scope the way the hue is.

    Two subtractions, two lines, and they must not be confused for each other. The
    subtitle says what the *reading* left out — the cap, and its numbers are the
    catalog's. The badge says what the *filters* are hiding, and it names both numbers
    whenever they are hiding any. A list that was capped and filtered and reported one
    number for both would be blaming the cap for the reader's own choice.

    The rows are SessionRows, not sessions, since the dashboards' delivery runs joined
    the list. A run file describes the same piece of work a session record does, from
    the other side — what it was and what it cost, rather than who and where and how
    long — and either side can be the one this PC still holds. So the two readings
    are made into one list (SessionRows.Of, matched by worktree and window), a run
    sits on its session's row, and a run no listed session drove is a row of its own
    that says so. One list, whichever half arrived first, and every control here works
    on rows without knowing which kind it is holding.

    The ✕ only reports that it was pressed; what that closes is the shell's business.
*@

<section @key=@("sessions-pane") class="sessions-panel" id="sessions-pane" aria-labelledby="sessions-title" data-testid="sessions-panel">
    @* The library's section header wearing this pane's own class names, subtitle
       test id included — the capped-list wording is asserted through it. *@
    <SectionHeader BaseClass="sessions-panel__header"
                   TextCssClass=""
                   EyebrowCssClass="sessions-panel__eyebrow"
                   TitleCssClass="sessions-panel__title"
                   DescriptionCssClass="sessions-panel__subtitle"
                   DescriptionTestId="sessions-subtitle"
                   ActionsCssClass="sessions-panel__header-actions"
                   Eyebrow="Sessions"
                   TitleId="sessions-title"
                   Title="Claude and Copilot"
                   Description="@Subtitle">
        <Actions>
            @* The library's badge wearing this pane's count class. It answers to the
               filters and not to the grouping: it stays put while Group by moves the
               same rows around, and it changes — and names both numbers — the moment
               Show or Machine leaves some of them out. *@
            <Badge BaseClass="sessions-panel__count" Kind="" TestId="sessions-count">@CountLabel</Badge>
            <IconButton AriaLabel="Close sessions pane" CssClass="btn--ghost" OnClick="() => OnClose.InvokeAsync()">✕</IconButton>
        </Actions>
    </SectionHeader>

    <div class="sessions-panel__filters" data-testid="sessions-filters">
        @* The two filters first, because they decide which rows exist before Group
           by decides where they sit — and reading left to right is the order the
           controls apply.

           A one-of-many strip rather than a Toggle, even though there are two
           options and a switch would fit. A role="switch" states a boolean
           *setting*, and this is not one: it resets to Live on every open, and a
           setting that silently forgets itself reads as broken. A strip that comes
           back to its first option reads as where you are looking, which is what
           this is — and it is exactly what Group by already does in returning to
           "Nothing". *@
        <div class="sessions-filter">
            <span class="sessions-filter__label">Show</span>
            <ButtonGroup AriaLabel="Which sessions to show" TestId="sessions-view-filter">
                @foreach (var view in Views)
                {
                    <ToggleButton Pressed="_view == view"
                                  PressedChanged="_ => ChooseView(view)"
                                  TestId="@($"sessions-view-{Slug(view)}")">
                        @AgentSessionViews.Label(view)
                    </ToggleButton>
                }
            </ButtonGroup>
        </div>

        <div class="sessions-filter">
            <span class="sessions-filter__label">Runs</span>
            @* A single toggle, not a strip: the question has one answer — only the
               rows with a delivery run on them, or every row — and it is a filter
               like Show, so the badge moves when it is pressed. *@
            <ButtonGroup AriaLabel="Whether to show only rows with a delivery run" TestId="sessions-runs-filter">
                <ToggleButton Pressed="_withRuns"
                              PressedChanged="value => _withRuns = value"
                              TestId="sessions-runs-only">
                    With runs
                </ToggleButton>
            </ButtonGroup>
        </div>

        <div class="sessions-filter">
            <span class="sessions-filter__label">Machine</span>
            @* "Machine", the dashboard's word for this same control, and not the
               column's "Environment". The two surfaces offer the same list — this
               device and every machine that has reported — and a reader who has
               narrowed the dashboard by machine should find the identical control
               under the identical name here rather than a second vocabulary for
               one filter. Environment stays the context's term everywhere the
               record is described: the column, the grouping, and the code. The
               library's SelectField in the dashboard's own machine-filter dress,
               so the two selects are one control rather than two that happen to
               look alike. *@
            <SelectField CurrentValue="@_environmentId"
                         Options="@EnvironmentOptions"
                         Label="Machine to focus on"
                         IncludeEmptyOption="true"
                         EmptyLabel="All machines"
                         CssClass="badge badge--area"
                         TestId="sessions-machine-filter"
                         OnChanged="ChooseEnvironment" />
        </div>

        <div class="sessions-filter">
            <span class="sessions-filter__label">Group by</span>
            @* A one-of-many strip over a closed set of three, which is what the
               dashboard's window filter is and for the same reason: the options are
               not a list somebody's configuration can lengthen. *@
            <ButtonGroup AriaLabel="How to group the sessions" TestId="sessions-grouping-filter">
                @foreach (var grouping in Groupings)
                {
                    @* PressedChanged rather than the pressed state itself: the strip
                       is one-of-many, so this pane owns the selection and the button
                       only announces the press. *@
                    <ToggleButton Pressed="_grouping == grouping"
                                  PressedChanged="_ => ChooseGrouping(grouping)"
                                  TestId="@($"sessions-group-{Slug(grouping)}")">
                        @GroupingLabel(grouping)
                    </ToggleButton>
                }
            </ButtonGroup>
        </div>

        <div class="sessions-filter sessions-filter--end">
            @if (_keeper is not null)
            {
                @* Amends every one of this machine's session records from what its
                   agents' files still hold, and asks sync to send them all again when
                   sync is on. Beside Refresh because it is the heavier version of the
                   same question: Refresh reads, this reads everything and writes it
                   down. *@
                <AppButton Variant="ButtonVariant.Secondary"
                           Disabled="@(_loading || _updating)"
                           TestId="sessions-update-records"
                           OnClick="UpdateRecordsAsync">
                    @(_updating ? "Updating records..." : "Update records")
                </AppButton>
            }
            <AppButton Variant="ButtonVariant.Secondary"
                       Disabled="@_loading"
                       TestId="sessions-refresh"
                       OnClick="RefreshAsync">
                @(_loading ? "Reading..." : "Refresh")
            </AppButton>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="sessions-panel__content" id="sessions-content">
        @if (_recordsMessage is not null)
        {
            <Alert Message="@_recordsMessage" BaseClass="sessions-panel__notice" Role="status" TestId="sessions-records-updated" />
        }
        @if (FocusMessage is { } focusMessage)
        {
            <Alert Message="@focusMessage" BaseClass="sessions-panel__notice" Role="status" TestId="sessions-focus-missing" />
        }
        @if (Unreadable.Count > 0)
        {
            @* Named, not swallowed. One agent's folder being unreadable leaves the
               other agent's rows perfectly good, and the reader has to be able to
               tell "no Copilot sessions" from "Copilot could not be read". *@
            <Alert Message="@UnreadableMessage" BaseClass="sessions-panel__notice" Role="status" TestId="sessions-unreadable" />
        }

        @* Total, not Shown: a refresh of a fully-filtered list has nothing on screen
           and is not a first read, and this message would flash on every one of them.

           The library's alert wearing this pane's own class, the same way the notice
           above it does. This was a hand-rolled p carrying that class, which is the
           copy nothing catches: the name is the pane's, so no rule about the
           library's names could see it. Adopting it changes no markup and no CSS,
           and it is how the line finally announces itself — a first read that says
           nothing to a screen reader is the accessibility cost the library exists
           to pay once. *@
        @if (_loading && Total == 0)
        {
            <Alert Message="Reading the sessions on this PC." BaseClass="sessions-panel__empty" Role="status" TestId="sessions-loading" />
        }
        else
        {
            @* The table's accessible name says "has a record of" rather than "on this
               PC", which is what the subtitle and the feature's own description
               already say. Some of these rows ran somewhere else, and the name of the
               table is not the place to claim otherwise. *@
            <DataTable TItem="SessionRow"
                       Columns="Columns"
                       Sections="Sections"
                       RowKey="@(row => (object)row.Key)"
                       RowCssClass="@(row => IsFocused(row) ? "sessions-table__row--focused" : null)"
                       RowAttributes="@FocusAttributes"
                       RowNoun="session"
                       AriaLabel="Claude and Copilot sessions this PC has a record of"
                       EmptyMessage="@EmptyMessage"
                       EmptyDescription="@EmptyDescription"
                       TestId="sessions-table">
                <Row Context="row">
                    @* The folder is the second line of the name rather than a column
                       of its own: it is the longest value in the row and the least
                       often the thing being looked for, and as a column it set the
                       table's width and pushed the dates off screen.

                       A replicated row has no folder to put there, and that absence
                       is not the one the em dash means. Or(...) says the agent
                       recorded nothing; here the agent recorded a folder and it
                       deliberately stayed behind — a raw path describes one machine's
                       disk and means nothing on this one, so ADR 0005 keeps it off
                       the wire entirely. Spending the em dash on it would file a
                       boundary this product chose under "nobody wrote it down", and
                       the two absences could no longer be told apart anywhere on this
                       screen — including on the one row where both occur at once,
                       which is a replicated session Claude recorded no repository
                       for.

                       So the line says what is true instead: which machine recorded
                       the session, and that its detail stayed there. That is also
                       this pane's only answer to what a reader tries next. There is
                       no transcript-opening control anywhere in this product, so
                       nothing here is disabled and nothing is greyed out — what would
                       read as broken is a row that looks exactly like a local one and
                       has nothing behind it, and naming the machine is what stops it
                       looking that way. The day a local row does grow a way into its
                       transcript, the sentence explaining why this row has none is
                       already written and already in the place a reader looks.

                       Prose in the library's own data-table__detail, and not
                       IntegrationUnavailable. That component states that a cluster of
                       acts cannot be performed, takes one of four causes — not
                       connected, not installed, offline, turned off in settings — and
                       draws its sentence in --color-text-disabled beside a glyph for
                       whichever one it was given. None of the four is true here:
                       nothing is unavailable, nothing is off, the record is complete,
                       and there is no act to disable. Forcing a cause on would make a
                       complete record read as a broken integration, which is the
                       exact misreading this line exists to prevent — and a p carrying
                       a paragraph and a glyph inside a table cell is the wrong weight
                       for a row besides.

                       Calm on purpose: no role, no colour of its own, no glyph.
                       Nothing happened and nothing is wrong, so it is a fact in the
                       row's own secondary ink at the weight the folder line already
                       has, because it is the same kind of detail. The machine's name
                       is in the text rather than in a tint, so the row survives
                       greyscale and reads out loud. *@
                    <td class="sessions-table__session">
                        <span class="@TitleClass(row)">@row.Title</span>
                        @if (row.RunOnly)
                        {
                            @* A row the runs alone account for: no session record on
                               this PC, only the dashboard's. The line says which
                               dashboard and under which key, so the reader knows what
                               the row rests on — the same job the origin line does
                               for a replicated session, and the same quiet register:
                               nothing is wrong with the row. *@
                            <span class="data-table__detail" data-testid="sessions-run-only">
                                <span class="data-table__clamp sessions-table__origin" title="@RunOnlyLine(row)">@RunOnlyLine(row)</span>
                            </span>
                        }
                        else if (row.Session!.Origin is AgentSessionOrigin.Replicated)
                        {
                            @* Clamped through the library's own class, exactly as the
                               folder line below is. The width cap alone is not enough
                               and was the bug: with no overflow rule the sentence ran
                               out of the cell and collided with the Type column, and
                               with no title there was no way to read the part that had
                               been cut. A machine name is as unbounded as a path, so
                               this line needs the same treatment paths get. *@
                            <span class="data-table__detail" data-testid="sessions-origin">
                                <span class="data-table__clamp sessions-table__origin" title="@OriginLine(row.Session)">@OriginLine(row.Session)</span>
                            </span>
                        }
                        else if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(row.WorkingFolder))
                        {
                            <span class="data-table__detail data-table__mono">
                                <span class="data-table__clamp" title="@row.WorkingFolder">@row.WorkingFolder</span>
                            </span>
                        }

                        @* A row answered from this machine's own record because the
                           agent's files no longer hold the session. Beside the folder
                           rather than instead of it — unlike a replicated row, the folder
                           is this disk's and still means something — and in the origin
                           line's quiet register, because nothing is wrong with the row:
                           it is as complete as the last reading that amended it, and the
                           sentence says that it cannot be read again. *@
                        @if (row.Origin is AgentSessionOrigin.Recorded)
                        {
                            <span class="data-table__detail" data-testid="sessions-recorded">
                                <span class="data-table__clamp sessions-table__origin" title="@RecordedLine">@RecordedLine</span>
                            </span>
                        }

                        @* The pull requests the session linked itself to, drawn the way
                           a run draws its own: the Integrations reference, "PR #n", an
                           anchor to GitHub. Only on a row with no run: where there is
                           one, they go to its line at the right, under State, with the
                           run's own (SessionRow.ReferencesFor), so a pull request is one
                           reference on screen and always in the same place. *@
                        @if (row.Runs.Count == 0 && row.PullRequests.Count > 0)
                        {
                            <span class="data-table__detail sessions-table__pull-requests" data-testid="sessions-pull-requests">
                                @foreach (var pr in row.PullRequests)
                                {
                                    <IntegrationLink Link="@Link(pr)"
                                                     ShowTitle="false"
                                                     CssClass="sessions-table__pull-request"
                                                     TestId="sessions-pull-request" />
                                }
                            </span>
                        }
                    </td>

                    @* The mark carries no title, because the word is right beside it
                       and the library's rule is that a labelled mark next to its own
                       label is announced twice. *@
                    <td class="sessions-table__kind">
                        <ProviderMark Provider="@Provider(row.Kind)" CssClass="sessions-table__mark" />
                        <span>@AgentSessionGroups.Label(row.Kind)</span>
                        @* Where the agent was run from, as a quiet outlined badge after
                           the word: a detail of the type, not a type of its own. *@
                        @if (EntrypointLabel(row.Session?.Entrypoint) is { } entrypoint)
                        {
                            <Badge Kind="kind"
                                   CssClass="sessions-table__entrypoint"
                                   Title="@($"Run from {row.Session!.Entrypoint}")"
                                   TestId="sessions-entrypoint">@entrypoint</Badge>
                        }
                    </td>

                    <td>@row.Environment</td>

                    @* Clamped for the same reason the folder is, and it is the state
                       column that pays for not doing it: a branch name in this product
                       is a slug with a hash on the end, and at 200 rows the longest one
                       set the table's width and pushed State off the right edge — the
                       one column a reader scanning for a running session actually
                       needs. The full value stays in the cell's title. *@
                    @* The recorded repository where there is one, and otherwise the
                       resolved one, marked as such: a reader can tell "the agent said
                       backlog" from "this folder is under the clone registered as
                       backlog" by the hint, and the title says which in words. The
                       em dash stays for a session with neither. *@
                    <td class="data-table__mono">
                        <span class="@RepositoryClass(row)" title="@RepositoryTitle(row)">@Or(row.Repository)</span>
                    </td>
                    <td class="data-table__mono">
                        <span class="data-table__clamp sessions-table__branch" title="@row.Branch">@Or(row.Branch)</span>
                    </td>
                    <td>@Moment(row.StartedAt)</td>
                    <td title="@Moment(row.LastActivityAt)">@Ago(row.LastActivityAt)</td>

                    @* Output and input, with every model and the cache figures in the
                       title. An em dash where the session did not record its usage —
                       Copilot never does — never a zero. *@
                    <td class="sessions-table__tokens" title="@TokensTitle(row.Session?.ModelUsage)" data-testid="sessions-tokens">@TokensCell(row.Session?.ModelUsage)</td>

                    @* The library's chip, so colour is never the only carrier of the
                       state — it brings its own glyph and its own word. *@
                    <td><IntegrationStateChip Session="@State(row.State)" /></td>
                </Row>

                @* The delivery runs on this row, under it and across every column.
                   A run is a thing that happened inside a session rather than beside
                   it — the session is the process, the run is the work it was
                   tracking — so it is not a column; and it is not the name cell's
                   third line either, which is where it started: a run's own line
                   carries a skill, a title, the work it links to and its outcome, and
                   a quarter of the table's width made three lines of each of them
                   while the other seven columns sat empty alongside.

                   Keyed the way the row is, so a re-read does not rebuild every fold
                   a reader had opened. *@
                <RowDetail Context="row">
                    @* Only where there is something to show: the fragment is asked
                       for every row, and a row with no run must not leave a stripe
                       under it. The wrapper is also what carries the cell's padding,
                       so an empty detail row is exactly nothing high. *@
                    @if (row.Runs.Count > 0)
                    {
                        <div class="sessions-runs">
                            @foreach (var run in row.Runs)
                            {
                                <DeliveryRunLine @key="@($"{run.Dashboard}/{run.Worktree}/{run.Id}")" Run="run" References="@row.ReferencesFor(run)" OnOpenTask="OnOpenTask" />
                            }
                        </div>
                    }
                </RowDetail>
            </DataTable>
        }
    </div>
</section>

@code {
    /// <summary>The views on offer, in the order the strip lists them. Live first,
    /// because it is the view the pane opens in.</summary>
    private static readonly AgentSessionView[] Views =
    [
        AgentSessionView.Live,
        AgentSessionView.All
    ];

    /// <summary>The groupings on offer, in the order the strip lists them. Ungrouped
    /// first, because it is the shape the list has when the pane opens.</summary>
    private static readonly AgentSessionGrouping[] Groupings =
    [
        AgentSessionGrouping.None,
        AgentSessionGrouping.Environment,
        AgentSessionGrouping.Kind
    ];

    /// <summary>The headings, in the order the row template writes its cells.</summary>
    private static readonly DataTableColumn[] Columns =
    [
        new("Session"),
        new("Type"),
        new("Environment"),
        new("Repository"),
        new("Branch"),
        new("Started"),
        new("Last activity"),
        new("Tokens"),
        new("State")
    ];

    /// <summary>An em dash: the agent did not record this. Distinct from an empty
    /// string, which would read as a value that happens to be blank.</summary>
    private const string Missing = "—";

    private AgentSessionCatalog _catalog = AgentSessionCatalog.Empty;

    /// <summary>The runs, and the one list the two readings make together. Built
    /// once per read rather than per render, because it hashes every session's folder
    /// against every worktree a run was filed under, and a render happens on every
    /// press in the filter strip.</summary>
    private DeliveryRunCatalog _runs = DeliveryRunCatalog.Empty;
    private IReadOnlyList<SessionRow> _rows = [];
    private AgentSessionView _view = AgentSessionView.Live;

    /// <summary>Whether only the rows with a delivery run on them are shown. Off on
    /// every open, like the view: a filter that remembered itself would open the
    /// pane on a subset the reader did not ask for.</summary>
    private bool _withRuns;
    private AgentSessionGrouping _grouping = AgentSessionGrouping.None;

    /// <summary>The environment the list is narrowed to, by id, or null for all of
    /// them. Held the way <see cref="_view"/> is — here, for as long as this pane
    /// lasts — and for the same reason: a new opening starts on every environment,
    /// and a stored choice would leave a reader looking at one machine's sessions
    /// without having asked for them.</summary>
    private string? _environmentId;

    private bool _loading;

    /// <summary>When the list was read, so "12 minutes ago" is measured against the
    /// reading rather than against the render. Neither strip re-reads anything: a
    /// grouping change and a view change are both a second render of one reading, and
    /// pinning the clock to the reading is what makes them say the same thing about
    /// the same row. Only Refresh moves this.</summary>
    private DateTimeOffset _readAt = DateTimeOffset.UtcNow;

    /// <summary>Raised when somebody dismisses the pane. Whether that returns to the
    /// workspace, opens something else or does nothing is the shell's decision — the
    /// pane only reports that the ✕ was pressed.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback OnClose { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Which identity colour a repository wears, asked per session, or null for one
    /// this pane's host cannot place.
    /// <para>
    /// A callback rather than a lookup of its own, for the reason this context holds
    /// <c>owner/name</c> as an opaque string in the first place: which repositories
    /// exist is a workspace question and answering it here would tie sessions to
    /// repository management. It is also the answer three other surfaces read, and a
    /// pane that worked its own out would be a second answer to which project is
    /// which. See <c>.devbook/design/color-scheme.md#band-identity-tokens</c>.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public Func<string?, int?>? RepositoryColour { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The repositories the shell has scoped, by alias, in the order taken; empty
    /// for all of them. Read on every render: the chips can move while the list is
    /// open, and the list follows them the way the dashboard's parts do.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public IReadOnlyList<string> RepositoryScope { get; set; } = [];

    /// <summary>
    /// What a session's recorded repository is called here — the configured alias
    /// for an <c>owner/name</c> or for an alias that arrived on the wire, or null
    /// for a repository this workspace does not know and for a session that
    /// recorded none. The shell's to answer, for the reason <see cref="RepositoryColour"/>
    /// is: the pane knows nothing about the repository list and should not have to.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public Func<string?, string?>? RepositoryAlias { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// How the shell opens a Backlog entry a delivery run was started from. Wired by
    /// the host because opening one is the workspace's business and not this
    /// context's: this pane holds sessions and runs, and an entry belongs to Tasks.
    /// <para>
    /// Without it a task reference renders as inert text rather than as a control
    /// that would have to refuse the click — the same tri-state the Integrations
    /// family applies to every reference nobody can follow.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<DeliveryRunReference> OnOpenTask { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The repository cell, plus its identity mark when the host placed it.
    /// <para>
    /// On the cell that names the repository rather than down the whole row: a row
    /// here is a session and the repository is one of eight things it reports, so a
    /// rule down its leading edge would read as if the row itself belonged to that
    /// project. Beside the name is also where the design rule wants it — the mark is
    /// never the sole carrier, and the name is right there.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private string RepositoryClass(SessionRow row)
    {
        var stem = row.RepositoryResolved
            ? "data-table__clamp sessions-table__repository sessions-table__repository--resolved"
            : "data-table__clamp sessions-table__repository";
        var colour = RepositoryColour?.Invoke(row.Repository);

        return colour is null ? stem : $"{stem} repo-mark repo-mark--{colour}";
    }

    /// <summary>The cell's title: the full value, and — for a resolved one — how
    /// it was arrived at, so the hint is never the only carrier. Which repository a
    /// row names, and whether it was recorded or placed, is the row's own answer:
    /// see <see cref="SessionRow.Repositories"/>.</summary>
    private static string? RepositoryTitle(SessionRow row) =>
        row.RepositoryResolved
            ? $"{row.Repository} — placed by its working folder, inside the registered clone. The agent recorded no repository."
            : row.Repository;

    /// <summary>
    /// The session's name, plus the family that says it is an identifier on a row
    /// that only has one.
    /// <para>
    /// A replicated record from a device that predates the title field names itself
    /// with its own session id. Rendering that in the ink and weight of a title
    /// somebody wrote is the row passing itself off as a named session: it reads as a
    /// name right up to the point somebody tries to make sense of it. The mono family
    /// is what this pane already gives every identifier in a row — the repository,
    /// the branch, the folder — so the id joins them rather than inventing a fifth
    /// treatment for the same kind of value. A record that carried its title (ADR
    /// 0005, 2026-09-23) shows it as a title.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private static string TitleClass(SessionRow row) =>
        row.Origin is AgentSessionOrigin.Replicated && string.Equals(row.Title, row.Id, StringComparison.Ordinal)
            ? "sessions-table__title data-table__mono"
            : "sessions-table__title";

    /// <summary>
    /// Where a replicated record came from, and that its detail stayed there. It
    /// takes the second line unconditionally on such a row: the folder is empty by
    /// contract, and a path that did somehow arrive would still name a disk this
    /// machine cannot open, so provenance is the truer thing to spend the one line
    /// on.
    /// <para>
    /// The machine's name and not its id. <c>EnvironmentId</c> is a sync device id —
    /// a different id space from this device's kernel identity, and nothing a person
    /// recognises. The fallback drops the name rather than the sentence, because
    /// "Recorded on ." is worse than a sentence admitting it cannot say which
    /// machine, and a name can be blank the way any wire field can.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private static string OriginLine(AgentSession session) =>
        string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(session.Environment)
            ? "Recorded on another machine. Its folder and transcript stay there."
            : $"Recorded on {session.Environment}. Its folder and transcript stay there.";

    /// <summary>What a row answered from this machine's own record says about itself:
    /// where it came from, and why the agent's files are not behind it.</summary>
    private const string RecordedLine =
        "From this PC's session record — the transcript is gone, so this is what the last reading kept.";

    /// <summary>A linked pull request as the Integrations family draws one — the same
    /// shape a run's pull request takes on the run's own line. No state chip, because
    /// nothing asked GitHub how it is doing.</summary>
    private static IntegrationLinkRef Link(AgentPullRequest pr) =>
        new(
            pr.Url,
            IntegrationProvider.GitHub,
            IntegrationLinkKind.PullRequest,
            $"PR #{pr.Number}",
            $"{pr.Repository}#{pr.Number}",
            pr.Url,
            Repository: string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(pr.Repository) ? null : new IntegrationRepositoryRef(pr.Repository, pr.Repository));

    /// <summary>
    /// Where the agent was run from, shortened for a badge: Claude's own prefix is
    /// dropped, since the Type column already says Claude — <c>claude-desktop</c> reads
    /// "desktop", <c>cli</c> stays "cli". Null where nothing was recorded; the title
    /// keeps the full spelling.
    /// </summary>
    internal static string? EntrypointLabel(string? entrypoint) =>
        string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(entrypoint)
            ? null
            : entrypoint.Trim() is var value && value.StartsWith("claude-", StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase) && value.Length > "claude-".Length
                ? value["claude-".Length..]
                : entrypoint.Trim();

    /// <summary>Output and input over every model, or an em dash where the session did
    /// not record its usage or recorded none.</summary>
    internal static string TokensCell(IReadOnlyList<AgentModelUsage>? usage) =>
        usage is { Count: > 0 }
            ? $"{MetricFormat.Compact(usage.Sum(model => model.OutputTokens))} out · {MetricFormat.Compact(usage.Sum(model => model.InputTokens))} in"
            : Missing;

    /// <summary>One line per model in the table's token words, and what the figure
    /// leaves out. Null where there is nothing recorded to break down.</summary>
    internal static string? TokensTitle(IReadOnlyList<AgentModelUsage>? usage) =>
        usage switch
        {
            null => null,
            { Count: 0 } => "No model usage recorded in this session.",
            _ => string.Join(
                "\n",
                usage.Select(model => $"{model.Model}: {DeliveryRunLine.TokenFigures(model.OutputTokens, model.InputTokens, model.CacheReadInputTokens, model.CacheCreationInputTokens)}")
                    .Append("This session's own spend; the agents it spawned are not included."))
        };

    /// <summary>
    /// What a run-only row rests on. The worktree's name is already the title; the
    /// line adds the dashboard and the key it filed the run under, which is the
    /// nearest thing to a folder the run knows — a path would be a guess, and the
    /// key at least identifies the folder to anyone holding the profile.
    /// </summary>
    private static string RunOnlyLine(SessionRow row) =>
        $"No session record on this PC. Recorded by {row.Runs[0].Dashboard} under {row.Runs[0].Worktree}.";

    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
    {
        _keeper = Services.GetService<ISessionRecordKeeper>();

        await RefreshAsync();

        // Every opening amends the records of the sessions that moved since the last
        // one, so a session is written down while its transcript is still there to
        // read. Not awaited by the list: the list is already read, and the records are
        // what a later reading falls back to, not what this one shows.
        if (_keeper is not null) _ = KeepRecordsAsync(_keeper);
    }

    private static async Task KeepRecordsAsync(ISessionRecordKeeper keeper)
    {
        try
        {
            await keeper.UpdateAsync(everything: false);
        }
        catch (Exception exception) when (exception is IOException or UnauthorizedAccessException)
        {
            // The next opening tries again; the list is unaffected either way.
        }
    }

    private ISessionRecordKeeper? _keeper;

    private bool _updating;

    private string? _recordsMessage;

    /// <summary>The update button: every session the files still hold, amended into
    /// its record — added to, never replaced — then the list read again so a record
    /// that now answers for a cleaned-away session shows.</summary>
    private async Task UpdateRecordsAsync()
    {
        if (_keeper is null) return;

        _updating = true;
        _recordsMessage = null;

        try
        {
            var update = await _keeper.UpdateAsync(everything: true);

            _recordsMessage = RecordsMessage(update);
        }
        finally
        {
            _updating = false;
        }

        await RefreshAsync();
    }

    internal static string RecordsMessage(SessionRecordUpdate update)
    {
        var what = update.Started + update.Amended == 0
            ? "No session files to read, so every record is as it was."
            : $"Updated {update.Amended} session record{(update.Amended == 1 ? "" : "s")} and started {update.Started}.";

        return update.Published
            ? $"{what} Sync will send every record again on its next cycle."
            : what;
    }

    private async Task RefreshAsync()
    {
        _loading = true;

        try
        {
            // Both readings in flight together: they are two folders read by two
            // readers, and neither needs the other's answer until both are in.
            var sessions = Sessions.GetSessionsAsync();
            var runs = Runs.GetRunsAsync();

            _catalog = await sessions;
            _runs = await runs;
            _rows = SessionRows.Of(_catalog.Sessions, _runs.Runs);
            _read = true;
            _readAt = DateTimeOffset.UtcNow;

            // A re-read can lose the environment the list was narrowed to — its only
            // records aged past the cap, or the source that held them could not be
            // read this time. Left in place, the select would fall back to showing
            // "All machines" over a list narrowed to nothing, which is the one
            // thing the control must not say. Widening back out is the honest reset.
            if (_environmentId is not null && Environments.All(environment => environment.Id != _environmentId))
            {
                _environmentId = null;
            }
        }
        finally
        {
            _loading = false;
        }

        RevealFocused();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// The session the pane was opened on — by a task that names it — or null.
    /// <para>
    /// The row is marked and receives focus, and the pane's own filters widen when
    /// they would hide it: the reader asked for this session, and "Live" hiding a
    /// finished one would answer with nothing. The shell's repository scope is left
    /// alone, because it is the shell's; a row outside it is said out loud instead.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? FocusSessionId { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The <see cref="FocusSessionId"/> last acted on, so re-rendering with
    /// the same one does not widen the filters the reader has since narrowed.</summary>
    private string? _focusApplied;

    private bool _focusPending;

    private bool _focusMissing;

    /// <summary>Whether the list has been read once, so an empty list is an answer
    /// rather than a reading still in flight.</summary>
    private bool _read;

    protected override void OnParametersSet()
    {
        if (string.Equals(FocusSessionId, _focusApplied, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase)) return;

        _focusApplied = FocusSessionId;
        _focusMissing = false;
        _focusPending = !string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(FocusSessionId);

        RevealFocused();
    }

    /// <summary>A row is the focused session when it is that session, or when one of
    /// its runs was driven by it — a run-only row still answers for the session id
    /// its run recorded.</summary>
    internal bool IsFocused(SessionRow row) =>
        !string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(FocusSessionId)
        && (string.Equals(row.Session?.Id, FocusSessionId, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase)
            || row.Runs.Any(run => run.SessionIds.Contains(FocusSessionId, StringComparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase)));

    private void RevealFocused()
    {
        if (!_focusPending || !_read) return;

        if (!ScopedSessions.Any(IsFocused))
        {
            _focusMissing = true;
            _focusPending = false;

            return;
        }

        if (!Visible.Any(IsFocused))
        {
            _view = AgentSessionView.All;
            _withRuns = false;
            _environmentId = null;
        }
    }

    private string? FocusMessage => _focusMissing && FocusSessionId is { } id
        ? _rows.Any(IsFocused)
            ? $"Session {id} is outside the repositories the header is scoped to."
            : $"This PC has no record of session {id}."
        : null;

    private static string RowDomId(SessionRow row) => $"sessions-row-{row.Key}";

    private IReadOnlyDictionary<string, object>? FocusAttributes(SessionRow row) =>
        IsFocused(row)
            ? new Dictionary<string, object>
            {
                ["id"] = RowDomId(row),
                ["tabindex"] = "-1",
                ["aria-current"] = "true",
                ["data-testid"] = "sessions-focused-row",
            }
            : null;

    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        if (!_focusPending || _loading) return;

        var row = Visible.FirstOrDefault(IsFocused);
        if (row is null) return;

        _focusPending = false;

        try
        {
            await JS.InvokeVoidAsync("backlogFocus", RowDomId(row));
        }
        catch (JSDisconnectedException)
        {
            // The pane went with its circuit; there is nothing left to focus.
        }
        catch (InvalidOperationException)
        {
            // Prerendering, or a test host with no JavaScript: the row is still
            // marked, which is the part a reader sees.
        }
    }

    /// <summary>Held here and nowhere else, so it lasts exactly as long as this
    /// pane does: closing and reopening is a new pane, and a new pane opens on
    /// Live. A stored view would make "what is going on right now" a question the
    /// reader had to remember having answered.</summary>
    private void ChooseView(AgentSessionView view) => _view = view;

    private void ChooseGrouping(AgentSessionGrouping grouping) => _grouping = grouping;

    /// <summary>One environment, or all of them when the empty option is chosen — the
    /// select hands back an empty string for that, and the module reads either as
    /// the absence of a filter.</summary>
    private void ChooseEnvironment(string? id) => _environmentId = string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(id) ? null : id;

    /// <summary>
    /// The environments the select offers: every one the reading holds a record of,
    /// read off the whole catalog and not off the view, so switching Show to Live
    /// does not take the option a reader just chose out from under them. Derived per
    /// render rather than stored, because it is a pure function of a catalog that
    /// only Refresh changes.
    /// </summary>
    private IReadOnlyList<AgentSessionEnvironment> Environments => AgentSessionEnvironments.Of(_rows);

    /// <summary>The environments as the library's select wants them: the id is the
    /// value and the name is the label, which is the whole reason an environment
    /// carries both.</summary>
    private IReadOnlyList<SelectorOption> EnvironmentOptions =>
        [.. Environments.Select(environment => new SelectorOption(environment.Id, environment.Name))];

    /// <summary>What the chosen environment is called, for the sentences that name it,
    /// or null when the list is not narrowed to one.</summary>
    private string? EnvironmentName =>
        _environmentId is null ? null : Environments.FirstOrDefault(environment => environment.Id == _environmentId)?.Name;

    /// <summary>The rows the filters admit, before any grouping. Environment, then
    /// view, then grouping: neither narrowing reorders, so which of the two goes first
    /// changes nothing, and the grouping never sees what either left out.</summary>
    /// <summary>Whether the header has narrowed the list to some repositories.</summary>
    private bool Scoped => RepositoryScope.Count > 0;

    private string ScopeLabel => string.Join(", ", RepositoryScope);

    /// <summary>A row in the scope: one whose repository — recorded by the agent,
    /// resolved from its folder, or named by a run's tracker item — the shell
    /// resolves to a scoped alias. Any of them will do: a row is about one
    /// repository however the product came to know which. With none there is nothing
    /// to resolve, so the row is out: an absence, not a guess.</summary>
    private bool InScope(SessionRow row) =>
        !Scoped || row.Repositories.Any(Scopes);

    private bool Scopes(string? repository) =>
        RepositoryAlias?.Invoke(repository) is { } alias
        && RepositoryScope.Contains(alias, StringComparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase);

    /// <summary>The list after the header's scope and before this pane's own
    /// filters: the scope decides which rows exist here at all, the way it does on
    /// the task list.</summary>
    private IReadOnlyList<SessionRow> ScopedSessions =>
        Scoped ? [.. _rows.Where(InScope)] : _rows;

    private IReadOnlyList<SessionRow> Visible
    {
        get
        {
            var rows = AgentSessionViews.Of(AgentSessionEnvironments.On(ScopedSessions, _environmentId), _view);

            // The same list back when the toggle is off, for the reason the view
            // hands the same list back for All.
            return _withRuns ? [.. rows.Where(row => row.Runs.Count > 0)] : rows;
        }
    }

    /// <summary>How many sessions the chosen environment has a record of, whatever
    /// the view. The number the empty state offers a reader as the way back out.</summary>
    private int OnEnvironment => AgentSessionEnvironments.On(ScopedSessions, _environmentId).Count;

    /// <summary>
    /// The sections to render, derived from the module's own grouping rather than
    /// worked out here. The pane translates the module's group into the table's —
    /// two records of the same shape in two projects, because the control library
    /// may not see a module and the module may not depend on a control library.
    /// The key crosses with the name: the grouping keys environments on their id,
    /// so two machines sharing a name are two sections, and the table has to key
    /// its rows on the same thing or it refuses to render the second.
    /// </summary>
    private IReadOnlyList<DataTableSection<SessionRow>> Sections =>
        [.. AgentSessionGroups.Of(Visible, _grouping)
            .Select(group => new DataTableSection<SessionRow>(group.Name, group.Rows, group.Key))];

    /// <summary>What the reading found. The subtitle's number and the second half of
    /// the badge's.</summary>
    private int Total => _rows.Count;

    /// <summary>What the view kept. The row count.</summary>
    private int Shown => Visible.Count;

    /// <summary>
    /// Both numbers whenever the view is hiding some, and one number when it is
    /// hiding none. Never "of" on a Live view over an all-live catalog: "2 of 2
    /// sessions" would send a reader looking for two rows that do not exist.
    /// <para>
    /// The noun agrees with the total on both branches, because the total is the
    /// number it is the noun for. A profile holding exactly one session record, over,
    /// opens on Live with nothing on screen — and "0 of 1 sessions" would be the badge
    /// disagreeing with the very number it is quoting.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private string CountLabel => Shown == Total
        ? (Total == 1 ? $"1 {Noun}" : $"{Total} {Nouns}")
        : (Total == 1 ? $"{Shown} of 1 {Noun}" : $"{Shown} of {Total} {Nouns}");

    /// <summary>What a row is called in the counts. "Session" while every row is one,
    /// and "session or run" once a run the list holds no session for has become a row
    /// of its own — a badge reading "522 sessions" over 200 sessions and 322 runs
    /// would be counting one thing under another's name.</summary>
    private bool AnyRunOnly => _rows.Any(row => row.RunOnly);

    private string Noun => AnyRunOnly ? "session or run" : "session";

    private string Nouns => AnyRunOnly ? "sessions and runs" : "sessions";

    /// <summary>
    /// Both numbers when the cap took something, and only the honest claim when it
    /// did not. "The most recent" on an uncapped list would be hedging about a list
    /// that is in fact complete.
    /// <para>
    /// This line describes the *reading* and deliberately not the view, which is why
    /// its count is the catalog's rather than the rows on screen. The cap and the
    /// view are two different subtractions; "the 2 most recent of 842" over a
    /// four-session reading filtered to two would be attributing one to the other.
    /// The badge is where the view's own subtraction is reported.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    /// <summary>The reading's own line, plus — while the header has narrowed the
    /// list — the one thing about that narrowing a reader cannot see from the
    /// rows: on what terms a session is in, so a Claude session that is missing
    /// can be understood as one outside every registered clone rather than as one
    /// that never ran.</summary>
    private string Subtitle
    {
        get
        {
            // The catalog's own numbers, not the row count: the cap is on sessions,
            // and the runs that became rows of their own were never under it.
            var reading = _catalog.Capped
                ? $"The {_catalog.Sessions.Count} most recent of {_catalog.Discovered} sessions this PC has a record of, most recently active first."
                : "The sessions this PC has a record of, most recently active first.";

            var runs = _rows.Count(row => row.RunOnly);

            if (runs > 0)
            {
                reading += runs == 1
                    ? " And 1 delivery run no listed session drove, as a row of its own."
                    : $" And {runs} delivery runs no listed session drove, as rows of their own.";
            }

            return Scoped
                ? $"{reading} Narrowed to {ScopeLabel}: a session is in by the repository its agent recorded, or by its working folder lying inside a registered clone; one with neither is left out."
                : reading;
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Nothing on this machine, or nothing this view admits — two different facts,
    /// and telling a reader with three finished sessions that there are none would
    /// be blaming the machine for their own filter.
    /// </summary>
    private string EmptyMessage => Total == 0
        ? "No sessions on this PC."
        : _withRuns && Shown == 0
            ? "No delivery runs here."
        : Scoped && ScopedSessions.Count == 0
            ? $"No sessions in {ScopeLabel}."
            : EnvironmentName is null
                ? "No live sessions right now."
                : $"No live sessions on {EnvironmentName} right now.";

    /// <summary>The description names both states out loud, and names the way back
    /// out. "Live" in the title above is only checkable if the sentence under it
    /// says which states that means and how many rows are on the other side.
    /// <para>
    /// Narrowed to one environment, the sentence names that environment and counts
    /// its sessions rather than the PC's: the way back out is still Show, because an
    /// environment is only ever offered when it has records behind it, so the view is
    /// the filter that emptied the list. "Recorded there" rather than "it has a record
    /// of", because for a machine the reader is not sitting at, it is this PC that
    /// holds the record.
    /// </para></summary>
    private string EmptyDescription => Total == 0
        ? "Neither Claude nor Copilot has left a session record in this profile yet."
        : _withRuns && Shown == 0
            ? "None of the rows this view admits carries a run an orchestration dashboard recorded. Turn off With runs to see them all."
        : Scoped && ScopedSessions.Count == 0
            ? "A session is matched to the scope by the repository its agent recorded, or by its working folder lying inside a clone registered for it — Claude records none, so its sessions need the clone. Clear the repository scope in the header to see every session."
        : EnvironmentName is null
            ? Scoped
                // The way back names the scoped count, not the catalog's: the
                // other view brings back the sessions in scope and no more.
                ? $"Nothing in {ScopeLabel} is running or stalled. Choose {AllLabel} to see the {SessionCount(ScopedSessions.Count)} recorded there."
                : $"Nothing is running or stalled on this PC. Choose {AllLabel} to see the {SessionCount(Total)} it has a record of."
            : $"Nothing on {EnvironmentName} is running or stalled. Choose {AllLabel} to see the {SessionCount(OnEnvironment)} recorded there.";

    private static string SessionCount(int count) => count == 1 ? "1 session" : $"{count} sessions";

    /// <summary>The other view's own word, read from the module rather than written
    /// out here. This is the one sentence in the product that names a view in prose,
    /// and it names the button the reader has to press — so it asks the button's
    /// label for it. "running or stalled" beside it stays literal: those are states,
    /// not the view. See <see cref="AgentSessionViews.Label"/>.</summary>
    private static string AllLabel => AgentSessionViews.Label(AgentSessionView.All);

    /// <summary>What could not be read, across both readings: an agent's folder,
    /// or a dashboard's. One list, because the sentence under the title is one
    /// sentence.</summary>
    private IReadOnlyList<string> Unreadable => [.. _catalog.Unreadable, .. _runs.Unreadable];

    private string UnreadableMessage =>
        $"Could not read {string.Join(" or ", Unreadable)} on this PC. Everything else below is complete.";

    private static string GroupingLabel(AgentSessionGrouping grouping) => grouping switch
    {
        AgentSessionGrouping.Environment => "Environment",
        AgentSessionGrouping.Kind => "Type",
        _ => "Nothing"
    };

    private static string Slug(AgentSessionView view) => view switch
    {
        AgentSessionView.All => "all",
        _ => "live"
    };

    private static string Slug(AgentSessionGrouping grouping) => grouping switch
    {
        AgentSessionGrouping.Environment => "environment",
        AgentSessionGrouping.Kind => "type",
        _ => "none"
    };

    private static IntegrationProvider Provider(AgentSessionKind kind) => kind switch
    {
        AgentSessionKind.Copilot => IntegrationProvider.Copilot,
        _ => IntegrationProvider.Claude
    };

    /// <summary>
    /// The three states this context can evidence, in the control library's
    /// vocabulary. The library's enum has more members and none of the extra ones is
    /// derivable from a file on disk, which is why the mapping is this narrow rather
    /// than clever.
    /// </summary>
    private static IntegrationSessionState State(AgentSessionState state) => state switch
    {
        AgentSessionState.Running => IntegrationSessionState.Running,
        AgentSessionState.Stalled => IntegrationSessionState.Stalled,
        _ => IntegrationSessionState.Finished
    };

    private static string Or(string? value) => string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(value) ? Missing : value;

    /// <summary>Local time, because a person comparing a session against their own
    /// morning is not thinking in UTC.</summary>
    private static string Moment(DateTimeOffset? moment) =>
        moment is null ? Missing : moment.Value.ToLocalTime().ToString("ddd d MMM HH:mm");

    /// <summary>
    /// How long ago, coarsely. The exact timestamp is in the cell's title, so this
    /// can round: down to the minute is what tells a reader whether a session is
    /// theirs from this morning, and no reader is comparing two sessions by seconds.
    /// </summary>
    private string Ago(DateTimeOffset moment)
    {
        var elapsed = _readAt - moment;

        if (elapsed < TimeSpan.Zero) return "just now";
        if (elapsed < TimeSpan.FromMinutes(1)) return "just now";
        if (elapsed < TimeSpan.FromHours(1)) return Plural((int)elapsed.TotalMinutes, "minute");
        if (elapsed < TimeSpan.FromDays(1)) return Plural((int)elapsed.TotalHours, "hour");

        return Plural((int)elapsed.TotalDays, "day");
    }

    private static string Plural(int count, string unit) =>
        count == 1 ? $"1 {unit} ago" : $"{count} {unit}s ago";
}
